From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brennoflavio <contato@brennoflavio.com.br>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 15:39:36 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] http2: align pseudo-header order and navigation priority

Serialize ordinary HTTP/2 pseudo-headers in Chromium's measured m,a,s,p
order without changing CONNECT's reduced header set or HTTP/1.1 requests.
Select weight 256 for non-internal document navigations and explicitly
reset other attempts to 16, using curl's existing stream-weight option.
The curl overlay uses that navigation weight to add the default ordinary
Priority: u=0, i only after HTTP/2 selection. Explicit Priority values,
including empty/suppression forms, win; fetches inherit no navigation
default. Classification is independent of caller-supplied Sec-Fetch headers.

nghttp2 1.70.0 discards the legacy priority specification accepted by
nghttp2_submit_request. Overlay its v1 request submission path (the one
curl uses) to pass navigation priority through the existing native HEADERS
encoder: exclusive, dependency zero, weight 256. Restore only this bounded
wire field, not the removed legacy priority scheduler or standalone PRIORITY
updates. Honor a peer's NO_RFC7540_PRIORITIES setting once received. Keep
body-provider copying and header ownership/error cleanup backend-owned.
Other submit APIs and non-navigation priority behavior remain unchanged.

Both source overlays are generated in the build cache with exact matches,
never by editing shared dependency packages. The generator is host-native
and the resulting C still uses the requested compilation target and flags.

This targets fingerprint recorder HTTP diagnostics, not the TLS verdict.
Use the same fresh Linux Chrome reference and temporary recorder workload
for navigation, JSON fetch, overrides, redirects, multiple streams and
responses larger than the stream/connection windows. HTTP/1.1 and no-ALPN
fallback must remain functional and omit only the generated default, not
caller-supplied Priority. No new upstream unit tests are added.

Full ordinary-header ordering, fetch priority/scheduler parity, HPACK
encoding, TLS/resumption and target-Mac evidence remain separate work.
References: fingerprint/README.md; docs/tls-patch-group-plan.md, group 4.
